The ARM census checks out: 468 unsafe mask expressions across 1,024 field pairs, including 465 pairs with negative widths; the 31 zero-width pairs are defined. Section D has separate record errors: its “three-state” table lists four states, and it narrates three distinct refused tasks. The supplied evidence cannot authenticate those prior runs or the claimed causes of refusal.

codex
No. The packet contains **wrong counts, a stale disposition, and conclusions presented as measurements that the supplied evidence does not establish**. It also does not consistently separate your judgment from a seat’s finding.

I read the five permitted files in the working `GXEMUL-SEC` tree. I did not read the archive or ledger, or run probes. “Confirmed” below means supported by the supplied excerpts or permitted source; it does not mean independently reproduced.

**A1/A2 contain the clearest factual errors.**

> “M8_texaccept … caught by all four store-first TEX rows”

**WRONG.** There are four TEX-specific rows in that detector, but only **two** store a TEX value before reading. The probe explicitly corrects this:

> “ON TWO OF THEM, NOT FOUR: `tex ld lo` and `tex ld hi`”

The other two are `tex rd lat1` and `tex xbit`. Their assertions include announcement behavior. As you already acknowledge, M8 also removes the announcement, so its **40/44** result does not isolate the contribution of storing first. [Probe source](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:399)

> “Is the bits 9/10 DISCLOSURE acceptable, or a breach?”

**WRONG as a current disposition.** The latest supplied evidence explicitly withdraws that disclosure:

> “So the disclosure was withdrawn and REPLACED BY COVERAGE.”

`CLASSVERIFY3.txt` reports:

> `BASE_fixed   46/46`  
> `M_bit9     45/46   KILLED`  
> `M_bit10    45/46   KILLED`

The current probe agrees: its setup checks cover bits **0–10**, and the expected result is **`1/11`**: one TEX announcement plus the presence of all eleven other announcement types. [Current assertion](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:491)

> “a TEX-read session never enters” the drawing-engine path

**WRONG without the historical restriction.** The final TEX test does enter that path. The probe describes “unreachable-in-session” as the **earlier** disclosure and explicitly retracts its rationale. [Correction](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:448)

The **bit enumeration itself is correct when attached to the right stage**:

| Bits | Meaning | Supplied measurement |
|---|---|---|
| 0–5 | Six RE width latches | One `M10_texbit` entry, **43/44** |
| 6 | DE width latch | **45/46**, killed in `CLASSVERIFY.txt` |
| 7 | Unmapped-write latch | **45/46**, killed in `CLASSVERIFY.txt` |
| 8 | Unmapped-read latch | **44/46**, killed in `L2VERIFY.txt` |
| 9 | Primitive-operation latch | Earlier **46/46** escape; later **45/46** kill |
| 10 | Unsupported-mode latch | Earlier **46/46** escape; later **45/46** kill |
| 11 | TEX-read latch under review | Its eleven other possible collision partners are bits 0–10 |

These assignments are present in the [enum](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:68).

Thus:

- Initially covering six partners leaves **five**: 6, 7, 8, 9, 10.
- Repairing bit 8 closes **one of those five**, leaving **four**.
- Widening through bit 8 covers **nine** partner bits.
- The final widening through bit 10 covers **eleven** partner bits.
- Comparing the edited latch against its partners is linear in their number. Both orderings of one partner remain one collision partner.

There is also an overclaim **inside the latest evidence’s commentary**:

> “each of the eleven other used bits … is killed — bits 0-5 by M10”

**OVERSTATED as a measurement census.** TABLE1 supplies **one M10 result**, not six separately enumerated width-bit results. The current source supports a coverage argument across those six bits, but that argument must be distinguished from six individual measurements. Likewise, the supplied runs do not show every variant rerun against the final instrument.

> “M4 … caught ONLY by … LAST-SLOT”; “M7 … caught ONLY by the last-slot row”

**UNSUPPORTED as measured row attribution.** The quoted table establishes **one failed row each**, through their **43/44** scores. It does not name those failed rows. The probe comments repeat the attribution and its structure supports the explanation, but the per-row measurement output is absent.

> “Under my original base-only design, M4 and M7 would both have swept clean.”

**OVERSTATED if presented as measured.** This is a counterfactual coverage argument. The original instrument and its executions are not supplied. Label it **source-derived prediction**, identifying the assumed original row set.

The other headline numerical results check out:

| Claim | Evidence and result |
|---|---|
| Pre-fix **30/44**, post-fix **44/44** | Exact supplied result lines; improvement of **14** passing rows |
| **12** TABLE1 mutants, all killed | Exactly twelve entries, all below 44/44 |
| TABLE1 baseline | Corrected header says **44/44**; **42/44** was M12’s score |
| Two added independence rows | Both report **1/1**; detector increases from **44** to **46** rows |
| Repaired baseline **46/46**, M13 **44/46** | Explicitly reported in `L2VERIFY.txt` |
| M13’s earlier **44/44** | **Predicted**, explicitly “not re-measured here” |
| Final detector has **46** rows | Current source count: 3 controls + 24 per-site + 2 cross-site + 10 accepted-read + 4 TEX + 1 catch-all + 2 independence |

The aggregate scores establish those totals. They do not independently establish binary provenance, an unchanged script, frozen-instrument identity, or which particular rows failed.

> “only 31 of 112 cells are reachable”

**Confirmed for the represented TEX window.** The array has 112 halfwords; the index advances through 28 starting positions and each write covers four consecutive cells. Their union is indices **0–30**, hence **31** cells. Adjacent register writes overlap in three cells. [Array](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:163), [write indexing](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:542)

The **factor-of-four discrepancy** is consistent with representing separate 64-bit registers as halfwords. Its architectural warrant is reported in a source comment; the hardware specification itself is not supplied.

> “`re_tex` has NO consumer”

**OVERSTATED literally; supported as “no functional consumer in this file.”** The debug statement reads the stored element. The source’s more precise explanation is “written and debug-printed, never read back.” Do not silently turn a whole-tree claim into a single-file claim. [Source](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:551)

**B1 confirms the dispatch defect, but not the entire consequence chain.**

The permitted Alpha source contains:

> `uint64_t a0 = ... , a1 = ...;`

and:

> `if (a0 >= 1)`  
> `cpu->invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, a1, INVALIDATE_VADDR);`

Its comment identifies selector **−2** as “invalidate everything” and **−1** as “invalidate everything with ASM=0.” Therefore the unsigned representations of both negative selectors take the VADDR branch. That is **confirmed**. [Declaration](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:165), [dispatch](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:309)

| Quoted claim | Classification and evidence |
|---|---|
| “`ALPHA_TBIA()` … is `alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)`” | **UNSUPPORTED within this audit.** Neither definition occurs in the permitted files. |
| “`vaddr_mask` defaults to `(int64_t)-1 on 64-bit”; “the alpha binding site” | **UNSUPPORTED.** Neither implementation nor binding is supplied. |
| “It therefore invalidates cached virtual page ZERO and nothing else.” | **UNSUPPORTED.** The dispatch proves a VADDR callback invocation. Establishing its exact effects requires the missing callback and binding, plus the claimed zero argument. |
| “Every other mapping survives”; the fast path “keeps consuming” `host_load`/`host_store` | **UNSUPPORTED.** The relevant Alpha cache and fast-path implementations are outside the supplied evidence. |
| “They are now verified” | **UNSUPPORTED as presented.** The asserted verification chain is missing. “Witness … NOT BUILT” is not itself disproof—static analysis can establish consequences—but that analysis must be supplied. |
| “source-confirmed by two seats independently … re-verified myself” | **UNSUPPORTED attribution/provenance.** No seat answers or verification excerpts are inlined. |

The proposed bounded-selector condition would route the negative selectors to `INVALIDATE_ALL`. Its stated limitation is also consistent with the source comment: it introduces no distinct ASM-selective handling. That establishes the **dispatch effect of the proposed change**, not complete TBI conformance.

Your **med → high** ranking is clearly marked as your judgment. The problem is the surrounding assertion that every premise supporting that judgment has now been verified.

**B2 has an important textual-versus-active-code distinction.**

> “EIGHT sites”; “LONE OUTLIER among nine return-value sites”; “eight precedents”

**OVERSTATED as executable precedents.** The literal search does return eight assignments, at lines **118, 204, 306, 339, 344, 356, 361, 383**. But **306, 356 and 383 are inside `#if 0`**.

The precise census is:

- **Eight textual** `ALPHA_V0` assignments.
- **Five active** assignments in the file.
- Of those five, one is in the PROM helper; four are in `alpha_palcode`.
- The active `swpctx` assignment writes `ALPHA_A0`.

The direct contradiction is real:

> `/* Return old context in v0. */`  
> `cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;`

Changing `A` to `V` is indeed **one character**. [Assignment](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:250), [first disabled assignment](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:299)

The return-value equality and the need to avoid coincidental equality with a stale `v0` are logically sound. The debugger’s `reg` capability, however, is **UNSUPPORTED here**: neither its implementation nor output is supplied.

> “The clobber is NOT a defect — in the wrapper `a0` is an output tied to an input”

**UNSUPPORTED for the wrapper; OVERSTATED as a general conclusion.** The wrapper is absent. If its constraints are as stated, they establish that **that wrapper** does not require preservation of incoming `a0`. They do not establish every caller’s expectations or the complete PAL register contract.

> “no in-tree dependency”

**UNSUPPORTED.** That requires a wider dependency review than these five files permit.

**B3 mixes supported implementation observations with broader conclusions.**

> “Twelve of sixteen encodable colour modes … one … `YCRCB 0xf0` is documented in `crmfbreg.h`.”

**UNSUPPORTED as the complete numeric/documentary claim.** The implementation has four explicit handled colour cases and a default. It extracts the field with:

> `color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`

But the mask definition and `YCRCB` definition are not in the permitted evidence. Four handled cases are confirmed; the **16**, **12**, and documented **0xf0** claims require the missing header. [Extraction and cases](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:210)

> “fills a buffer with `random()` and writes it to guest memory”

**OVERSTATED.** The source declares **`buf[8]`**, assigns random values only to **`buf[0]` through `buf[3]`**, and calls `memory_rw` with **`bufdepth`**, which can be 1, 2, 4 or 8. It does not fill every byte of the buffer or necessarily every byte submitted for writing. The call also follows earlier coordinate, TLB-mode and tile-pointer checks. [PUT implementation](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:307)

> “there is NO mode validation: the store is unconditional and unmasked”

**OVERSTATED.** The narrow supported statement is: **accepted four- or eight-byte register writes do not validate the colour-mode encoding in the MODE_DST case**. The handler checks access width; the helper masks fields and checks other mode/address conditions. “Unconditional” needs that scope. [Register writes](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1134), [MODE_DST case](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1195)

> “So the witness IS constructible.”

**UNSUPPORTED by that premise alone.** Absence of validation at the register write is one part of a reachability argument. The packet does not supply the complete argument. This should be a stated source-derived conclusion with its assumptions, not a measurement.

> “the function has NO common tail, and `fatal()` does not exit”

The first part is **confirmed for `horrible_getputpixel`**, which ends after its PUT/GET branches. It is false if generalized to the enclosing device handler, which has a common tail. The second part is **UNSUPPORTED as a general function claim** because `fatal`’s implementation is absent; the PUT branch contains no explicit `exit` call. [Helper ending](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:381)

> “NEVER an early return”; “The primary vector must be an UNDOCUMENTED value.”

These are **design judgments**, not measurements. The stated singleton-coverage concern supports finding the documented-value-only design insufficient. It does not establish that selecting an undocumented primary value is the uniquely required or sufficient design.

The **five seats**, **5/5 agreement**, and **three of five** detector-hole attribution are all **UNSUPPORTED** without their answers.

**B4’s implementation finding needs a narrower PC claim.**

> “the decoder does not exclude source field 15”

**Confirmed.** The decoder selects `bfi`, passes `&cpu->cd.arm.r[rm]`, and has no special exclusion for 15 in that branch. [Decoder](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:4564)

> “`r[15]` is NOT the PC in this interpreter”

**OVERSTATED.** The supported distinction is that **this register slot is not maintained as the current execution PC on this path**. The source says:

> “`r[15]` is not maintained during execution.”

Translation reconstructs the instruction address into `cpu->pc`; other PC-sensitive paths use separate temporary storage. This supports rejecting an assumed current-PC value, not a universal assertion that the register slot has no PC role. [Explanation](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:1189), [translation](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:3967)

